00:00:03.139 --> 00:00:05.210 align:start position:0%
in 1995 three doctors quit their jobs to  make a videogame they had no game  development experience had recently  graduated med school and $100,000  between them this modest startup was  none other than Bioware the studio  behind later games such as Mass Effect  Knights of the Old Republic and of  course  Baldur's Gate  when Bioware was founded computer  role-playing games were in a bit of a  dry spell young development talent was  geared toward first-person shooters and  real-time strategy games in the late 90s  leveraging the improved graphics at the  time RPGs were abundant in the console  space with Chrono Cross Xenogears and  the Final Fantasy games still going  strong but aside from Diablo in 1996 and  the original fallout two years after  that  Baldur's Gate was an anomaly an RPG  created for the computer crowd inspired  by the pen and paper games Bioware  members had grown up with yet looking  back it's held as one of the games that  saved computer RPGs it exemplified  player freedom fluid storylines and an  interconnected world cut to 2000 and we  have Baldur's Gate 2 shadows of aam it  expanded on the tenets of its  predecessor in nearly every way it made  Bioware a household name for PC players  I still remember the first time I played  it like many I was entranced by its  complex setting its memorable storyline  it's phenomenal side quests and depth of  strategy I still remember a de LAN the  dragon and John irenic hissed the  villain  I still remember replaying the game as  soon as I finished it and just recently  I picked up divinity original sin 2 and  like many I can't help but see the  similarities between this game and  Baldur's Gate 2 both are sequels that  expand on their predecessors in nearly  every way both reward your imagination  both are masterpieces and both actually  encourage role-playing something the  genre has ironically lacked on many  occasions in the past decade  you can point to something like Mass  Effect or Dragon Age Origins games where  you create a hero and build them toward  a variety of set endings based on  choices you make along the way you  oftentimes decide to be good or evil  heroic or villainous and these games are  very good at doing so but that's the  thing these games are often centered  around binary choices do you follow the  Paragon route and spare the Solarians  life or do you follow the renegade path  plunging the galaxy into chaos at every  turn  [Music]  there's a definite dichotomy to the  decisions you're making whether the game  makes any attempt to hide it or not the  best moments of modern RPGs are Sophie's  Choice scenarios situations when you  have to sacrifice one character to spare  another or when you have to choose  between the good of the many or the more  emotional good of the few they're not  based on some concrete morality meter  but rather which characters ideal is  aligned with yours or which soldier will  be most useful in the grand scheme of  things don't be afraid  [Music]  [Applause]  there she is Burma foul which no one is  burning anyone friends let me put it  this way these types of games give you  chaotic worlds that you must then  introduce order to whether that be  through heroic deeds evil acts or some  blend of the two your choices have  specific effects on the world your story  is branching in many directions but  there are only a few real endings you  can arrive at another example is The  Witcher 3 Wild Hunt its side quests  explore the politics racism and fear of  a fantasy world murdering a king could  come back to bite you down the road  supporting one character's ascent to  political office could have unforeseen  effects in the future it constantly  presents you with chaos and asks you to  make sense of it all divinity original  sin - on the other hand does things  differently in fact it almost does the  exact opposite it gives you an orderly  world with concrete rules and asks you  to be the agent of chaos you can kill  anyone you want recruit anyone you want  betray anyone you want original sin - is  brilliant not because it presents you  with specific choices to make but  because it lets you create the choices  yourself my undead character bleaker  encounters a clairvoyant lizard who can  help me escape from prison he can also  help my friend the red Prince regain his  throne he also has beef with my elf  companions Sabeel both of my comrades  want to talk to the lizard in private  away from prying ears in a lesser game  this might be a simple matter of right  or wrong good or bad but I just met this  lizard and I don't know what kind of  ruler the Prince would make down the  road and Sabeel just stuck a needle in  my neck only hours ago should I play it  safe and focus on escaping or allow my  friend some time alone with the NPC the  game can branch in dozens of directions  at this point but it's a superb  role-playing game because in my mind the  choice is much more complicated than it  needs to be  that's because true to the genres name  I'm role-playing I created my skeletal  character with a threatening ravenous  zombie warrior in mind what I failed to  consider was that my morbid appearance  would make normal everyday conversations  difficult for me so of course I put a  bucket on my head problem solved but of  course now I look like an idiot  and idiots don't make the most  threatening heroes from there on out I  saw my character as a bumbling underdog  who means  but can't help providing comic relief at  every turn in fact I fell so deeply into  this character that when he missed an  easy shot or accidentally lit his  teammates on fire it made sense in a  super dumb kind of way and it was okay  this world is so vast and interconnected  and dense I can always find another way  to make things work I think that's the  heart that pumps vitality into every one  of Divinity's systems its world  influences your choices and not the  other way around the fascinating  byproduct of this is that I don't always  have a solid idea of who my character  actually is and if you'll allow me to be  dramatic here for a second isn't that  usually how things go in the real world  how often do we as humans make mistakes  or out of character choices or poor  judgment calls how often do we do  something stupid and can't do much but  live with the consequences Larry ins RPG  is remarkable in the way that actually  encourages mistakes and I think that's  the secret here by allowing for  coincidence and accidents original sin -  makes your deliberate choices all that  much more powerful knowing that things  could fall apart at any moment makes it  all that much more special when you're  holding them together and when you  inevitably screw up divinity is right  there to say it's okay  [ __ ] happens try to make the best of it  try to build something from the ashes  you'll inevitably load previous saves  throughout the game and in a computer  RPG I highly encourage you to do so but  this game is at its best when you roll  with the punches like pillars of  eternity and torment tides of numenera  original sin - continues the legacy of  baldur's gate - and the pen and paper  games that inspired it they're games  that actually encourage role-playing  they allow you to imagine a character  yes but they also encourage you to  reconsider that character along a way to  play a role but to also toy with it to  target the strings and watch the entire  thing unravel in the most magnificent  way  [Music]  you
 

